A pointer-to-pointer keeps the link that points to the current node. This makes the head case and the middle-of-list case use the same code.

The loop stops at the first node whose data equals `X`. If no node matches, the list stays unchanged.

```cpp
/*
Definition of singly linked list:
class ListNode{
  public:
    int data;
    ListNode *next;
    ListNode() : data(0), next(nullptr) {}
    ListNode(int x) : data(x), next(nullptr) {}
    ListNode(int x, ListNode *next) : data(x), next(next) {}
};
*/

class Solution {
public:
    ListNode* deleteNodeWithValueX(ListNode* &head, int X) {
        ListNode** link = &head;

        while (*link != nullptr && (*link)->data != X) {
            link = &((*link)->next);
        }

        if (*link != nullptr) {
            *link = (*link)->next;
        }

        return head;
    }
};
```

For example, in `4 -> 7 -> 9` with `X = 4`, `link` starts at `head`, so changing `*link` removes the first node directly. The same operation works for any later node.

Time complexity: `O(n)` in the worst case.
Space complexity: `O(1)`. The input list is changed in place.

This keeps the previous node and the current node while scanning the list. Once `current` has the required value, `previous->next` is changed to skip it. The head is checked first because it has no previous node.

Only one node is deleted. Later nodes with the same value are left as they are.

```cpp
/*
Definition of singly linked list:
class ListNode{
  public:
    int data;
    ListNode *next;
    ListNode() : data(0), next(nullptr) {}
    ListNode(int x) : data(x), next(nullptr) {}
    ListNode(int x, ListNode *next) : data(x), next(next) {}
};
*/

class Solution {
public:
    ListNode* deleteNodeWithValueX(ListNode* &head, int X) {
        if (head == nullptr) {
            return nullptr;
        }

        if (head->data == X) {
            head = head->next;
            return head;
        }

        ListNode* previous = head;
        ListNode* current = head->next;

        while (current != nullptr) {
            if (current->data == X) {
                previous->next = current->next;
                break;
            }
            previous = current;
            current = current->next;
        }

        return head;
    }
};
```

A useful edge case is a one-node list. If its value is `X`, the head becomes `nullptr`; otherwise it remains unchanged. An empty list is also safe.

Time complexity: `O(n)` in the worst case.
Space complexity: `O(1)`. The list structure is changed directly.

This version handles the head separately, then looks at `current.Next`. That means `current` is always the node before the possible match, so removal only needs one link update.

If `X` does not appear, the loop reaches the end and returns the original head.

```go
/*
Definition of singly linked list:
type ListNode struct {
    Data int
    Next *ListNode
}
*/

func deleteNodeWithValueX(head *ListNode, X int) *ListNode {
    if head == nil {
        return nil
    }

    if head.Data == X {
        return head.Next
    }

    current := head
    for current.Next != nil {
        if current.Next.Data == X {
            current.Next = current.Next.Next
            return head
        }
        current = current.Next
    }

    return head
}
```

Dry run with `1 -> 4 -> 4 -> 6` and `X = 4`: the first check sees that the head is not `4`. Then `current` is `1`, so its `Next` node is removed. The result is `1 -> 4 -> 6`.

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(1)`, since no extra list or recursion is used.

A dummy node before the real head avoids writing a separate removal block for the first node. I walk with `previous` and `current`, then skip the first matching node by changing one `next` link.

The dummy node is temporary and is not added to the returned list. If the list is empty, `head` is returned as `null`.

