## Intuition

In a singly linked list, a node cannot be removed directly because there is no backward link.
To delete a node with value `X`, we must **find that node and also keep track of the node just before it**.
Once the previous node is known, deletion is done by **changing one pointer to skip the target node**.
The only special case is when the node to be deleted is the **head**, because it has no previous node.

---

## Approach

* First, check if the **head node** contains value `X`. If yes, delete it by returning `head->next`.
* Use two pointers:

  * `ptr1` to traverse the list and find the node with value `X`
  * `ptr2` to keep track of the previous node
* Traverse the list until `X` is found or the list ends.
* If `X` is not found, return the original head.
* If found, update `ptr2->next` to `ptr1->next` to remove the node from the list.
* Return the updated head.

---

## Solution

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    ListNode* deleteNodeWithValueX(ListNode* &head, int X) {
        // Edge case: empty list
        if (head == nullptr) return head;

        // Edge case: delete head
        if (head->data == X) {
            return head->next;
        }

        ListNode* prev = head;
        ListNode* curr = head->next;

        // Traverse to find the node with value X
        while (curr != nullptr && curr->data != X) {
            prev = curr;
            curr = curr->next;
        }

        // If X not found, return original head
        if (curr == nullptr) {
            return head;
        }

        // Delete the node by skipping it
        prev->next = curr->next;
        curr->next = nullptr;

        return head;
    }
};
```

---

